NAME
    install.sh - safely copy a staged m-boot UEFI image to prepared mounts

SYNOPSIS
    ./install.sh --stage DIR --esp DIR --data DIR [--apply]
                 [--allow-directory]

DESCRIPTION
    This standalone Linux installer accepts two existing, separate mountpoints
    (vfat ESP with the full loader and exFAT payload partition) and a build.sh stage for x86_64-efi
    or arm64-efi. It never formats, partitions, calls dd or grub-install, or
    writes to raw block devices. Existing EFI fallback files are never
    overwritten. An existing m/boot/loader directory is reused only when
    its recursive contents on the ESP match the staged loader exactly; if different,
    installation fails without merging or replacing existing files.
    The exFAT mount is validated but contains no m-loader; it is reserved
    for external tools, bootable ISO images, virtual machines and other
    large payloads such as backups. Built-in loader files (including
    theme, configuration and GRUB modules) are installed on the ESP only.
    The default mode is a no-write plan. --apply is required to copy files.
    If a file copy fails after writing begins, partial files may remain;
    the error explicitly reports that possibility.

OPTIONS
    --stage            Required build.sh output directory for one target.
    --esp              Required already-mounted FAT32 ESP root directory.
    --data             Required already-mounted exFAT data root directory.
    --apply            Explicitly copy missing files (default: dry run).
                       An identical already-installed loader is not recopied.
    --allow-directory  Bypass mount/fs checks for disposable test directories
                       ONLY; this is not real-media validation.
    --help             Print usage.

EXAMPLES
    ./install.sh --stage ./dist/x86_64-efi --esp /mnt/esp --data /mnt/data
    ./install.sh --stage ./dist/x86_64-efi --esp /mnt/esp --data /mnt/data --apply

LIMITATIONS
    This tool does not install BIOS boot sectors. i386-pc stages require a
    separately reviewed disk installation procedure, not a file copy.
    A second EFI architecture can be installed on the same ESP if the new
    fallback filename is unused and the ESP loader is identical.
    Updating/replacing files is a separate task requiring deliberate backup
    and confirmation semantics.
    Preparation of the initial partitions is a separate safety-gated task.

SEE ALSO
